1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,701
Episode 29.

3
00:00:04,701 --> 00:00:07,065
Lights in the Deep Ocean.

4
00:00:07,065 --> 00:00:09,782
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,782 --> 00:00:13,885
Does a glowing ocean animal need a battery-powered light bulb?

6
00:00:13,885 --> 00:00:15,312
Ready?

7
00:00:15,312 --> 00:00:17,391
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,391 --> 00:00:20,951
The museum has a simulated deep-ocean ride.

9
00:00:20,951 --> 00:00:25,720
Maya, Leo, and Pip sit in a pretend research submarine.

10
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:31,086
The screen outside their window grows darker as the imaginary vehicle descends.

11
00:00:31,086 --> 00:00:33,872
Then a small blue light appears.

12
00:00:33,872 --> 00:00:37,690
Someone forgot to turn off a lamp, Leo says.

13
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:44,035
A narrator explains that many ocean organisms can produce light through chemical reactions.

14
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:47,323
The process is called bioluminescence.

15
00:00:47,323 --> 00:00:49,402
Pip is impressed.

16
00:00:49,402 --> 00:00:52,241
Where do they plug in their chargers?

17
00:00:52,241 --> 00:00:56,711
They do not need electrical sockets like you, Maya says.

18
00:00:56,711 --> 00:00:59,755
The screen shows different examples.

19
00:00:59,755 --> 00:01:03,097
A fish has a glowing lure near its mouth.

20
00:01:03,097 --> 00:01:07,622
Another animal produces flashes that may confuse an attacker.

21
00:01:07,622 --> 00:01:12,214
Some animals use light to help them blend with faint light from above.

22
00:01:12,214 --> 00:01:16,005
So light can do different jobs, Leo says.

23
00:01:16,005 --> 00:01:18,627
It is not just decoration.

24
00:01:18,627 --> 00:01:24,891
The ride pauses at a question: Does every glowing creature make light in exactly the same way?

25
00:01:24,891 --> 00:01:27,078
Maya chooses no.

26
00:01:27,078 --> 00:01:34,388
The narrator explains that some animals make light themselves, while others receive help from glowing bacteria.

27
00:01:34,388 --> 00:01:39,864
Pip designs a submarine with a large illuminated sign: Friendly visitors.

28
00:01:39,864 --> 00:01:42,187
No snacks for sale.

29
00:01:42,187 --> 00:01:45,869
That would make us easy to notice, Leo says.

30
00:01:45,869 --> 00:01:49,388
Then I will add an off switch, replies Pip.

31
00:01:49,388 --> 00:01:53,586
When they leave the exhibit, daylight fills the museum hall.

32
00:01:53,586 --> 00:01:56,494
Maya looks back at the dark screen.

33
00:01:56,494 --> 00:02:02,038
A place can be dark, she says, and still contain many kinds of light.

34
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:04,945
Now, a little more to discover.

35
00:02:04,945 --> 00:02:10,489
Bioluminescence is light produced by chemical reactions in living systems.

36
00:02:10,489 --> 00:02:17,608
Marine organisms use it in different ways, such as attracting prey, communicating, or defense.

37
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:21,331
Some animals depend on light-producing bacteria.

38
00:02:21,331 --> 00:02:27,418
The glowing lure of an animal is not an electric bulb, even if it looks like a tiny lamp.

39
00:02:27,418 --> 00:02:30,258
Let us hear the main idea again.

40
00:02:30,258 --> 00:02:34,483
The pretend submarine introduced living lights in the ocean.

41
00:02:34,483 --> 00:02:40,692
Different organisms used light for different purposes, and some had bacterial partners.

42
00:02:40,692 --> 00:02:46,019
The children separated a useful lamp comparison from the actual biology.

43
00:02:46,019 --> 00:02:48,994
Here are three useful expressions.

44
00:02:48,994 --> 00:02:52,119
You can listen, or say them with me.

45
00:02:52,119 --> 00:02:54,524
What is making that light?

46
00:02:54,524 --> 00:02:56,929
What is making that light?

47
00:02:56,929 --> 00:02:59,470
Some use it to find food.

48
00:02:59,470 --> 00:03:02,010
Some use it to find food.

49
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:04,646
Others use it for protection.

50
00:03:04,646 --> 00:03:07,282
Others use it for protection.

51
00:03:07,282 --> 00:03:09,877
Remember our listening question?

52
00:03:09,877 --> 00:03:13,980
Does a glowing ocean animal need a battery-powered light bulb?

53
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:16,114
Here is the answer.

54
00:03:16,114 --> 00:03:17,595
No.

55
00:03:17,595 --> 00:03:23,573
Bioluminescence involves chemical reactions, sometimes with help from bacteria.

56
00:03:23,573 --> 00:03:27,228
And here is a question just for your imagination.

57
00:03:27,228 --> 00:03:32,241
If you could send one message with a pattern of lights, what would the pattern mean?

58
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:35,353
There is more than one possible answer.

59
00:03:35,353 --> 00:03:38,600
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
